WEBVTT

00:00:00.673 --> 00:00:02.493
Punctuation traps.

00:00:02.493 --> 00:00:04.283
When not to punctuate.

00:00:04.283 --> 00:00:05.608
No rule?

00:00:05.608 --> 00:00:06.631
No punctuation.

00:00:08.181 --> 00:00:10.106
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:10.106 --> 00:00:13.071
One choice has no punctuation at all.

00:00:13.071 --> 00:00:15.634
And there's often a long subject before the verb.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.402
It's testing whether you can resist punctuation that isn't needed.

00:00:21.469 --> 00:00:22.974
Here's the method.

00:00:22.974 --> 00:00:28.249
Name the parts on each side of the blank: subject, verb, object.

00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:32.969
If they belong together, like a subject and its verb, use no mark.

00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:36.134
Then ask: what rule allows this mark?

00:00:36.134 --> 00:00:37.909
If you can't name one, cut it.

00:00:38.976 --> 00:00:42.876
Rule one: nothing goes between a subject and its verb.

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:45.251
Not even after a long subject.

00:00:45.251 --> 00:00:45.921
No comma.

00:00:47.006 --> 00:00:51.531
Rule two: nothing goes before that, after a verb like found.

00:00:51.531 --> 00:00:54.356
Found, comma, that is wrong.

00:00:54.356 --> 00:00:55.174
Just found that.

00:00:56.243 --> 00:01:00.628
Rule three: nothing goes after such as, or including.

00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:02.538
Not such as, colon.

00:01:02.538 --> 00:01:04.227
Just such as oak and maple.

00:01:05.263 --> 00:01:06.738
Let's try one.

00:01:06.738 --> 00:01:12.368
This whole chunk, from the researchers to the Canadian Arctic, is one long subject.

00:01:12.368 --> 00:01:16.473
Then comes the verb, concluded, and then what they concluded.

00:01:16.473 --> 00:01:20.303
These parts belong together, so nothing should separate them.

00:01:20.303 --> 00:01:22.703
A comma before that would split them.

00:01:22.703 --> 00:01:26.458
A comma before concluded would split the subject from its verb.

00:01:26.458 --> 00:01:29.533
And a colon right after a verb is never right.

00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:31.973
So we use no punctuation at all.

00:01:31.973 --> 00:01:32.980
The answer is A.

00:01:34.039 --> 00:01:36.099
Watch out for the breath comma.

00:01:36.099 --> 00:01:38.919
A long subject makes you want to pause.

00:01:38.919 --> 00:01:39.889
Don't.

00:01:39.889 --> 00:01:42.097
A pause when you read aloud isn't a rule.

00:01:43.148 --> 00:01:49.868
Here's the whole lesson on one page: how to spot it, the three steps, the rules, and the trap.

00:01:49.868 --> 00:01:52.687
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
